{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 49",
  "problem": "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{n-3}-{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}={a}_{2}={a}_{3}=1,\\,n\\ge 4.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},\u2026,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+\u2026+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},\u2026,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-3}-{a}_{n-1}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$  and ${a}_{n-3}$ in the relation is $3.$ So, the relation has order $3.$\n\n- The previous terms ${a}_{n-1},$ and ${a}_{n-3}$ occur only in the first power and their coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, yet there is no forcing function (i.e., $g(n)=0$ for all $n$). So, the relation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$3,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{homogeneous}.$",
